introduction:
A robotic dexterous hand’s morphology sets the limits of what any controller can do with it. Finger length, root placement, thumb opposition, joint axes, fingertip geometry and palm shape together decide whether contacts can be made, adjusted and remade before the object falls [1]. No learning algorithm recovers a contact transition the mechanism forbids. Building such a hand requires time-consuming expert work. The designer cycles between a geometric concept, a URDF or MJCF implementation, collision debugging, a stable initial grasp, dynamic tests, and reinforcement learning (RL). A model that looks reasonable can still hide a misoriented axis, self-interference, a narrow grasp basin, or an initial state that holds only because two bodies overlap. These faults particularly affect users who know their manipulation requirements but are not specialists in hand design.

Language is a natural interface here, though words alone do not establish mechanical validity. What matters is the supporting workflow: parameterized source code, physical tests, a fixed learning protocol, and a result package. We design this workflow as an agent tool: Task2Hand Skill — an instruction file plus generators, validators, and evidence schemas that a general-purpose coding agent follows.

The gates split the loop by each side’s responsibilities. The agent handles what is checkable by machine, including generation, validation, bounded search, training, and analysis, and runs autonomously within declared bounds. When the evidence exposes a trade-off, a plateau, or conflicting signals across gates, the Skill escalates to a human who sets the next design direction. Every escalation and answer is written to the ledger, so the design path is replayable. We characterize the boundary between autonomous and human-guided operation empirically in Sec. III.

Three questions follow. RQ1: Can an AI coding agent turn a non-expert brief into an executable, physically admissible hand by way of reusable gates, and do those gates protect validated capabilities during subsequent iteration? RQ2: Does the morphology accepted through the interactive loop achieve higher rotation success under a fixed, matched protocol? RQ3: Which conclusions hold across objects, and does temporal tactile input change them?

Our test case is bidirectional in-hand manipulation, which demands signed rotation authority, object retention, coordinated contact release, and lateral fingertip motion. Starting from a four-finger layout, a user named functional problems and a coding agent wrote every model, validator, search script, training interface, and analysis artifact. The resulting design process produced a task-specialized dexterous hand, which we call SAGE Hand (Skill-Aware Generative Embodiment), together with the failure cases from which we distilled the Skill (Fig. 1).

Our contributions are:

a reusable Task2Hand contract that maps a natural-language requirement and a bounded parameter space to URDF/MJCF, contact-grounded gates, RL packaging, and a provenance ledger;

an interaction protocol with named escalation conditions, at most three costed options per escalation, and a recorded answer, together with an empirical characterization of the boundary between autonomous and human-guided operation;

an evidence-driven design trajectory for bidirectional in-hand rotation that yielded SAGE Hand: a four-finger dexterous hand with 16 actuated DoF, an integrated guiding palm, compact roots, cylindrical tips, and a collision-cleared opposable thumb;

a matched evaluation on three objects in which SAGE Hand achieves a higher success rate than the reference hands, simulated tactile input more than halves the cylinder drop rate, and a controlled iteration study showing that the Skill path preserves capture at 164/165 trials while the path without the Skill degrades to 14/165.

Given a specialized Skill and a bounded number of human decisions at evidence-defined points, an agent can carry a contact-rich morphology design from a brief to a hand model with a trained controller and auditable design records.

1. a reusable Task2Hand contract that maps a natural-language requirement and a bounded parameter space to URDF/MJCF, contact-grounded gates, RL packaging, and a provenance ledger;

2. an interaction protocol with named escalation conditions, at most three costed options per escalation, and a recorded answer, together with an empirical characterization of the boundary between autonomous and human-guided operation;

3. an evidence-driven design trajectory for bidirectional in-hand rotation that yielded SAGE Hand: a four-finger dexterous hand with 16 actuated DoF, an integrated guiding palm, compact roots, cylindrical tips, and a collision-cleared opposable thumb;

4. a matched evaluation on three objects in which SAGE Hand achieves a higher success rate than the reference hands, simulated tactile input more than halves the cylinder drop rate, and a controlled iteration study showing that the Skill path preserves capture at 164/165 trials while the path without the Skill degrades to 14/165.

Robots that fold, lift, or spread cloth would benefit from a simulator that behaves like the specific object in front of them. Physical digital twins reconstructed from video provide such simulators: they recover the geometry, dynamics, and appearance of an object from a recorded interaction, and can then resimulate that interaction, predict its continuation, or render the outcome of new actions (Jiang et al., 2025). Simulations of this kind are already used to evaluate robot policies without physical trials (Zhang et al., 2025). How faithful a twin can be depends on two modeling choices: the physical representation of the object, and the way contact with the robot enters that representation.

Thin deformable objects make both choices difficult. Cloth resists stretching but bends easily, so its shape is dominated by curvature, and a gripper acts on it through a small patch of material that is captured, clamped, and later released. PhysTwin (Jiang et al., 2025) handles both with one spring–mass system, whose particles represent the object and also connect it to the gripper. We present HermiteTwin, a digital twin for thin deformable objects that represents deformation and contact separately, each at its own resolution (Figure 1). Its physical state is a coarse bicubic Hermite shell (Ni et al., 2024), a smooth (C1C^{1}) surface on which Kirchhoff–Love bending is defined directly and has its own stiffness. Contact, in contrast, is resolved in material space: the gripper captures, clamps, and releases dense material samples, each of which acts on the shell through the Hermite basis. The grasp can therefore be resolved finely while the shell itself stays coarse.

From multiview video of a robot interaction, we reconstruct the object’s rest shape and a Gaussian appearance model (Kerbl et al., 2023), identify effective material and contact parameters by inverse simulation, and render the simulated shell by moving the Gaussians with it. Grasp events make the identification objective nonconvex and only piecewise smooth, so we combine global search with CMA-ES (Hansen & Ostermeier, 2001) and local refinement with adjoint gradients.

We evaluate HermiteTwin on real robot manipulation from the Deform360 dataset (Li et al., 2026), comparing it with PhysTwin and the learned point-based simulator GS-Dynamics (Zhang et al., 2024). HermiteTwin reproduces the observed motion more accurately in 3D and in images and predicts the continuation of the recorded interactions more accurately. On a diagnostic interaction, transferring our grasp model to PhysTwin’s particles does not recover the observed fold, which suggests that the shell representation, not only the grasp model, contributes to the difference. The identified twins can also be driven by new actions, such as draping the cloth over a book or placing it inside a box. In summary, our contributions are:

a physical digital twin for thin deformable objects built on a bicubic Hermite shell, a compact C1C^{1} state with explicit Kirchhoff–Love bending;

a material-space grasp model that captures, clamps, and releases dense material samples and couples them to the shell, so that the grasp is resolved finely on a coarse shell;

an identification pipeline combining CMA-ES with adjoint refinement, and an evaluation on real manipulation data showing that HermiteTwin resimulates observed motion and predicts future frames more accurately than spring–mass and learned baselines.

1. a physical digital twin for thin deformable objects built on a bicubic Hermite shell, a compact C1C^{1} state with explicit Kirchhoff–Love bending;

2. a material-space grasp model that captures, clamps, and releases dense material samples and couples them to the shell, so that the grasp is resolved finely on a coarse shell;

3. an identification pipeline combining CMA-ES with adjoint refinement, and an evaluation on real manipulation data showing that HermiteTwin resimulates observed motion and predicts future frames more accurately than spring–mass and learned baselines.
